        overview: `If you're looking for a tournament facility that feels like it was designed with travel baseball families in mind, the USSSA Space Coast Complex in Viera delivers. This sprawling 100-acre campus features 13 all-turf fields that drain beautifully after Florida's infamous afternoon storms, meaning your games rarely get delayed for long. The complex sits about 45 minutes south of Orlando, giving you access to theme park adventures without being right in the tourist chaos. Parents rave about the covered seating areas and the fact that you can actually see your kid's game from the concession stands. The facility hosts major USSSA tournaments year-round, so you'll be playing against quality competition. The turf fields provide consistent playing surfaces that reduce bad hops and keep gameplay fair across all diamonds. For families who've dealt with mud-soaked cancellations elsewhere, this place feels like a baseball paradise.`,
        whatToExpect: `When you roll into USSSA Space Coast Complex for a tournament weekend, plan to be impressed by the organization. Check-in is typically smooth, with tournament staff directing you to parking areas designated by age group or field assignments. The 13 turf fields are arranged in a fan-friendly layout, making it relatively easy to catch multiple games if you have players on different teams or want to scout upcoming opponents. Between games, you'll find clean restrooms throughout the facility—a detail that matters more than you'd think after a full day in the Florida sun. The concession stands offer standard ballpark fare: hot dogs, hamburgers, nachos, and cold drinks. Pro tip: bring a cooler with extra water and snacks because tournament days are long and prices add up fast. The covered pavilion areas are first-come, first-served, so arriving early pays off. Many families set up pop-up tents along the outfield fences where shade is scarce. The turf fields heat up significantly in summer months, so be prepared with cooling towels and plenty of hydration for your player. Tournament schedules are usually posted the Wednesday before weekend events, giving you time to plan your logistics.`,
        whereToStay: `Viera and nearby Melbourne offer plenty of hotel options for tournament families. The Holiday Inn Melbourne-Viera Conference Center is a popular choice, located just minutes from the complex with a pool for post-game wind-down. The Homewood Suites and Hampton Inn in the Viera area both offer breakfast included, which saves money and morning stress. If you're traveling with multiple families, consider renting a vacation home through VRBO—the Space Coast area has some great options with pools and enough rooms for two or three families to split costs. For budget-conscious families, the La Quinta Inn and Fairfield Inn provide solid accommodations without breaking the bank. Most hotels are within a 10-15 minute drive of the complex, and you'll find plenty of restaurants and grocery stores nearby for stocking up on tournament essentials.`,
        whereToEat: `Tournament families quickly discover Long Doggers, a local favorite known for massive portions and a casual, kid-friendly atmosphere. For a sit-down dinner after games, Squid Lips Overwater Grill offers waterfront dining with fresh seafood and great sunset views. If your crew wants familiar chains, the Viera area has everything from Chili's to Olive Garden along the main commercial strip. For quick breakfast before early games, First Watch has become a tournament family go-to. Craving authentic Florida seafood? Head to Grills Seafood Deck in Port Canaveral, about 20 minutes away, for shrimp and fish tacos with an ocean view. The complex also has on-site concessions, but many families pack coolers with sandwiches and snacks to save money and eat between games without leaving their setup.`,
        thingsToDo: `You're just 45 minutes from Orlando's major theme parks if you want to extend your trip into a family vacation. But for something more unique to the area, Kennedy Space Center Visitor Complex is an absolute must—kids can see real rockets, meet astronauts, and experience space simulators. The beaches of Cocoa Beach and Melbourne Beach are about 20 minutes east, perfect for a morning surf lesson or afternoon shell hunting. For nature lovers, the Brevard Zoo offers kayaking through animal habitats and a treetop adventure course. If your player needs extra practice, there are several batting cage facilities in the area. The Exploration Tower in Port Canaveral offers panoramic views and interactive exhibits, making it a good rainy-day backup plan.`,
